  "articles": [
    {
      "sequence_id": 1,
      "text": "            Unit 7's notes cover two important points: the personal 'a' and the noun 'muebles'. \nWhen the direct object of a verb is a person or personified animal, Spanish uses the preposition 'a' before the object, called the personal 'a'. \nFor example, 'Veo a mi amigo' (I see my friend). \nThe noun 'muebles' means 'furniture' but is a count noun in Spanish, so 'un mueble' means 'a piece of furniture'. \nUnderstanding these differences helps avoid literal translations that sound unnatural in Spanish.\n"
    }
